<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miyamoto Musashi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1584–<a href="/years/1645">1645</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miyamoto Musashi was a Japanese swordsman, strategist and artist, famed for fighting some sixty duels without defeat, including his contest with Sasaki Kojiro in 1612. He developed the two-sword style known as Niten Ichi-ryu. In his last years he lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumamoto">Kumamoto</a> under the patronage of the Hosokawa lords, where he wrote The Book of Five Rings and painted ink works.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miyamoto-musashi.jpg" alt="Miyamoto Musashi" width="330" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miyamoto_Musashi-Portrait-Edo-period.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The details of Musashi's early life are difficult to verify. In The Book of Five Rings he says he was born in the village of Miyamoto, and an early biography, the Niten Ki, gives the year as 1584. His father, Shinmen Munisai, was an accomplished martial artist, a master of the sword and the jutte, and his mother, Omasa, died giving birth to him. He gives his full name as Shinmen Musashi-no-Kami Fujiwara no Harunobu, and he was also called Shinmen Takezō and Niten Dōraku, his Buddhist name. Accounts say he never took a wife, cut his hair or entered a bath.</p><p>By his own account his first duel took place at the age of 12 or 13, against Arima Kihei, and he won. At 16 he beat Tadashima Akiyama, and in 1599 he left his village. At 21 he defeated several students of a sword school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1600 Musashi probably served in the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuroda-yoshitaka">Kuroda Yoshitaka</a> on the Eastern side. A popular theory put him with the Western Army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a>, but recent research suggests that he and his father were on the Eastern side, although whether he fought at Sekigahara or at the battle of Ishigakihara in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a> is still debated. In 1608 he met the Tokugawa general Mizuno Katsunari and taught him the secrets of his sword style. He is said to have served in the Tokugawa army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-osaka">Siege of Osaka</a> in 1614 and in the suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimabara-rebellion">Shimabara Rebellion</a> in 1638.</p><p>His best-known duel was with Sasaki Kojirō on Ganryū Island, arranged through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosokawa-tadaoki">Hosokawa Tadaoki</a>. Musashi, it is said, deliberately arrived late and had carved an oar into a wooden sword, and he killed Kojirō with one vertical stroke. From 1633 he stayed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosokawa-tadatoshi">Hosokawa Tadatoshi</a> at Kumamoto Castle, and in 1640 he became a retainer of the Hosokawa lords.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1641 Musashi wrote the Hyōhō Sanjūgo, the &quot;Thirty-five Instructions on Strategy&quot;, for Tadatoshi, which became the basis of The Book of Five Rings. In 1643 he retired to the cave Reigandō and wrote that book, finishing it in the second month of 1645. A week before his death he gave it, with the Dokkōdō, &quot;The Path of Aloneness&quot;, to his student Terao Magonojō. He died around 13 June 1645, and sources disagree on whether it was in the cave or at a house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumamoto">Kumamoto</a>. He developed the two-sword technique Niten Ichi-ryū, and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a> and ink paintings, recognised as masterpieces, in the style called sumi-e.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miyamoto Musashi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tadatoshi hosted the swordsman <b>Miyamoto Musashi</b> in his last years.</blockquote><a href="/state/kumamoto-domain">Kumamoto Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harima-province">Harima Province</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.97,134.67&amp;year=1623" aria-label="Harima Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7964 899 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Miyamoto Musashi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8414 1169h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8414 1169h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.97,134.67&amp;year=1623">What was here in 1623? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
